02:10:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand where you are coming from. The logic makes intuitive sense: if we possess the capability to prove extinction with 100% certainty for one animal, then absolute proof exists, and we shouldn't lower our scientific standards just because another animal is harder to find. However, the reason scientists *must* carve out exceptions is a matter of pure physical reality. The standard of proof used for the Dodo cannot be applied to an insect because the physical constraints of the Earth make it impossible. To see why biologists have to use different standards, consider this analogy: **The Elephant vs. The Ant** Imagine I ask you to verify, with 100% certainty, that there is no fully grown elephant in your living room. You can walk in, look around, and say, "I am 100% certain there is no elephant here." You have verified it. No ifs, ands, or buts. Now, imagine I ask you to verify, with 100% certainty, that there is not a single **carpenter ant** anywhere inside your house. To get the exact same level of 100% certainty that you had with the elephant, you couldn't just look around. You would have to rip open every wall, tear up the floorboards, dismantle the plumbing, and inspect every square inch of insulation at the exact same time (so the ant couldn't move from one room to another while you were looking). Unless you completely destroy the house to check every microscopic crevice, you cannot be 100% certain. You can only say, "I set traps, I see no ants, I am 99.9% sure." This is the exact problem scientists face, but on a planetary scale. ### Why the Dodo is the Exception, Not the Rule The Dodo and the Passenger Pigeon are the "elephants in the living room." We could verify their extinction because they were large, and their "living room" (a small island, or a zoo cage) was completely within our field of vision. But insects, fungi, and small amphibians are the "ants in the walls." Except the house isn't a house—it's the 2.5-