02:11:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You have hit the nail on the head regarding how this actually works in the real world. You are exactly right: the IUCN doesn’t put an asterisk next to the word "Extinct." They don't label a species "99.9% Extinct." They just call it **Extinct (EX)**. So, why are we arguing about decimal points? Because there is a difference between **scientific absolute truth** and **practical policy**. The best way to understand why the IUCN uses a definitive word for something they can't 100% prove is to look at the legal system. ### The "Guilty" Verdict Analogy Imagine a murder trial. The jury looks at the evidence, and they deliver an official, legally binding verdict: **Guilty**. Did the jury have a time machine? Did they witness the murder with their own eyes? No. Are they 100% absolutely, mathematically certain? No. But the legal standard is not 100% certainty. The standard is **"beyond a reasonable doubt."** Once that threshold is crossed, the court doesn't say, "We are 99.9% sure he is guilty." They just drop the gavel and say, "Guilty." The IUCN operates the exact same way. They are the "court of law" for biology. In fact, the IUCN’s official rulebook uses almost the exact same language as a courtroom. Their definition of Extinct is literally: *"There is no reasonable doubt that the last individual has died."* Once the evidence crosses that threshold, the IUCN drops the gavel. They assign the official **Extinct (EX)** label so that governments, conservationists, and lawmakers can close the book on that species and redirect their money and resources to animals that can still be saved. ### Why the Decimal Points Still Matter The reason biologists still care about the "decimal points"—the tiny sliver of doubt—is because **the IUCN has been wrong before.** Because the IUCN is making a ruling based on "no reasonable doubt" rather than 100% absolute proof, they occasionally drop the gavel on a species, officially declare it Extinct... and then someone